<h2>How the Imposition Sheet Yield Calculator works</h2><p>This calculator tests two simple rectangular impositions: all pieces in their normal orientation and all pieces rotated 90 degrees. Available sheet size is reduced by the entered edge margin, then the tool uses floor((available dimension + gutter) ÷ (piece dimension + gutter)) to count columns and rows. The higher-yield orientation is reported.</p><h2>How to use this imposition sheet yield calculator</h2><p>Enter the usable parent-sheet size, the imposed piece size including any bleed you want reserved, the gutter between pieces and an edge margin for areas that cannot be occupied. The result shows both orientation counts so a prepress operator can see whether rotation alone improves yield before opening dedicated imposition software.</p><h2>How to interpret the result</h2><p>Pieces per sheet is only the first production question. The approximate unused-area percentage helps reveal whether a layout that technically fits leaves significant waste. However, material waste and press economics also depend on makeready, grain direction, finishing, color bars, gripper edge and whether offcuts can be reused.</p><h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2><p>The tool does not solve mixed-orientation nesting, irregular shapes, folding signatures, work-and-turn/work-and-tumble layouts, packaging dielines or gang runs. It assumes a simple axis-aligned grid. For press-ready imposition, use the printer’s workflow and constraints; this utility is for fast estimating and feasibility checking.</p><h2>Practical example and workflow</h2><p>On a 700 × 1000 mm sheet with 10 mm edge margins, a 216 × 303 mm piece and 5 mm gutters, the calculator compares the two grid orientations. An estimator can use the best count to estimate required sheets, then hand the job to prepress for final imposition and press-specific marks.</p>
